WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.644 --> 00:00:23.134
I think especially here,
in the heart of Silicon Valley,

00:00:23.134 --> 00:00:28.500
where organizations pride themselves on
their innovation and their creativity.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:35.000
If any place were to be able to take
advantage of a diverse workforce and

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.710
the creativity that's associated with
that diversity, it should be here.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:44.160
And yet time and time again,
we see evidence that Silicon Valley firms

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.270
are in fact very homogeneous,
mostly male and mostly white.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:55.920
We don't have a lot of leaders who
are female or people of color.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.720
But let's focus on a minute on females,

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.900
because if we're ever going to
be successful in diversity.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.320
That's should be the dimension
on which we're most successful,

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.215
because we have the most of them, right?

00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:12.093
51% of the population, so, what happens?

00:01:12.093 --> 00:01:15.779
Well, turns out that we
actually have a bias.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.810
If we think leader,
too many of us think male.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.280
And if we think male, we think leader.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.843
But in fact, there's some very
interesting research that's coming out

00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:34.974
in the last couple of years, that suggests
that that perspective may be changing.

00:01:34.974 --> 00:01:36.569
[MUSIC]

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:40.479
One of my most favorite studies,
is a study that was done, is actually

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.480
a meta-analysis, so they got a whole bunch
of other studies, 99 different studies.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.761
And they looked at leaders
who evaluated themselves and

00:01:48.761 --> 00:01:51.004
whose subordinates evaluated them.

00:01:51.004 --> 00:01:55.540
And they came up with a very
interesting set of results.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:00.380
What they found was,
that while male leaders evaluated

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.230
themselves as more effective than
did their female counterparts.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:11.000
The subordinates of this leaders,
evaluate the female leaders

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.320
as more effective than they
did their male counterparts.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:24.690
And this was true, except in two
industries government and the military.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.060
Now, what's unique about
those two industries?

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.910
Those two industries
are extremely hierarchical.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.390
So, women were perceived by their
subordinates as better leaders

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.150
in organizations or

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:41.180
in industries where it was a more
collaborative form of leadership.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.550
Other researchers have found
that one of the ways to actually

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:50.490
get a more effective group is
to increase the number of women.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.648
And it turns out there's actually a very
high correlation between the number of

00:02:54.648 --> 00:02:58.632
women in a group and the ability of that
group to be innovative and creative.

00:02:58.632 --> 00:03:03.764
[MUSIC]

00:03:03.764 --> 00:03:08.030
The first contender may be that there
simply aren't enough qualified women.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.460
A second contender maybe none conscious or
explicit bias.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.510
A third contender maybe that
the criteria by which we

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:25.020
hire changes depending
on who we're looking at.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.316
And the fourth criteria maybe in
fact what happens is we get women

00:03:31.316 --> 00:03:36.293
into the organization,
we don't move them up because we change

00:03:36.293 --> 00:03:40.274
the criteria by which we
judge their performance.

00:03:40.274 --> 00:03:41.430
[MUSIC]

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:45.081
This is an interesting perspective,
because it suggests that were there enough

00:03:45.081 --> 00:03:47.400
qualified women, of course,
we would hire them.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:53.805
But what's interesting to observe,
given that statement, is that since

00:03:53.805 --> 00:04:01.390
1988 more women have graduated from
US universities than have men.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:08.320
And in 2010, for example,
the majority of people receiving

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.250
undergraduate degrees,
masters degrees, and PhD's were women.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.720
And so it turned out that a lot of people
who are getting these advanced degrees

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.400
happen to be female.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.220
So maybe it is not that there
are not enough qualified women.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.320
Maybe what is happening is we are not
seeing these women as qualified.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.780
Now, maybe that is because
of implicit bias, right?

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:38.866
We have biases about what we think would
be the correct person having this job.

00:04:38.866 --> 00:04:42.089
And so my favorite example of how powerful

00:04:42.089 --> 00:04:46.330
this implicit bias is is some
work by Golden and Rouse.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.360
And what they did was,

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.700
they went to symphony orchestras
who were conducting auditions.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:57.160
And they said, we'd like you to conduct
your auditions behind a curtain.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.060
Now this didn't seem a big deal,
because think about a symphony orchestra.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.290
What does a symphony orchestra care about?

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:07.990
The quality of the music.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:14.130
Who cares if the violin that is so awesome
is being played by a male or a female?

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.400
This is irrelevant.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.890
And in fact, certainly their financial

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.940
model does not require that they
have all males or all females.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:26.016
It's irrelevant, it's the quality of
the music that the patrons will pay for.

00:05:26.016 --> 00:05:29.790
And so what happened?

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:34.420
So they did their
auditions behind a curtain

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:39.530
which hid the race and
gender of the musician.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:46.964
And when they did that, women were 50%
more likely to move up in the audition and

00:05:46.964 --> 00:05:51.830
250% more likely to gain
a seat in the orchestra.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:59.310
So while there seems to be no economic
incentive as to why we have this bias.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.716
It maybe that there is
a physiological impediment that stops

00:06:05.716 --> 00:06:11.135
us from seeing from hearing
the music of women as being equal or

00:06:11.135 --> 00:06:14.697
sometimes better than the music of men.

00:06:14.697 --> 00:06:18.176
[MUSIC]

00:06:18.176 --> 00:06:22.784
Wouldn't that be awesome if we could
actually just judge people on their

00:06:22.784 --> 00:06:27.694
performance rather than filtering that
performance through their notion of

00:06:27.694 --> 00:06:31.660
gender and what is appropriate or
not appropriate.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.550
Well, actually, an organization
here in the Valley did that.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:39.870
What they did is,
they had an online application process and

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:44.500
the application process required
that people engage in activities

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:49.030
that were representative of what they
would be doing in the organization.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.400
And before they judged who they
would actually bring in for

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.720
the interview they stripped all
of the information about race or

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.350
gender from the applicants,
they just have their performance.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:06.740
And when the curtain was
pulled back over 60% of

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:11.932
the top performers were people of color or
women.

00:07:11.932 --> 00:07:14.110
Their's an organization here called,

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:18.820
Speak with the Geek which is
a tech recruiting organization.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.810
And they did an interesting experiment.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:29.730
They sent out resumes to their clients,
5,000 resumes, and

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:34.990
the clients review those resumes and then
sent back requests to interview folks.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.590
And when they did this with full
information about the race and

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:42.050
gender of the applicants.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.740
Fewer than 5% of the women were
called back for interviews.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.770
They took those same 5,000 resumes and
sent them out again,

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:59.390
but they stripped off identifying
information about race and gender.

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:04.800
And in that case, 54% of the resumes
that were asked to be brought in for

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.930
interviews were resumes of women.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.451
The difference is huge.

00:08:09.451 --> 00:08:14.449
[MUSIC]

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:18.668
Oftentimes we look at the candidate and
decide what it is we want, and

00:08:18.668 --> 00:08:21.450
adjust the criteria to
who we are looking at.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:29.210
There was a wonderful study that
was done by researchers who posed

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:35.600
to people who were doing recruiting for
a traditional male job, a police chief.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.450
And in advance of their
looking at candidates,

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:41.900
they ask the recruiters what
do you care more about?

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.820
And it turns out there were two
dimensions that really came through.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:48.930
One was experience, and one was education.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:56.460
And so in fact, these recruiters
privileged education over experience.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:03.760
So they sent out two vitas,
the vitas were exactly the same,

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:11.580
except that vita number one, from Bob,
had more education than experience.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.940
And from Barbara,
she had more experience than education and

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:20.549
true to what they said,
they selected Bob over Barbara.

00:09:23.006 --> 00:09:25.980
Then what the researchers did is,
they switched the names.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:31.520
So now Barbara has more education and
Bob has more experience, and

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.160
guess what the recruiters preferred now?

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.520
You guessed it, experience.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.600
The solution here of course is,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.389
to establish clear criteria before
you begin the interview process.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:45.760
But that's just half the job.

00:09:46.772 --> 00:09:51.420
You've gotta make sure that you
actually have the correct criteria.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.680
So consider for example, the challenge
that Carnegie Mellon University had

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:03.450
when they attempted to improve the gender
balance of their computer science major.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:08.350
They were having a very small
percentage of women applying for and

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.520
being admitted to their
computer science program.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:17.840
Now as they reassessed the criteria by
which they judged whether you could

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:23.859
be admitted or not, one of the criteria
was a lot of experience in computers.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.910
Which at first blush,
seems quite reasonable, computer major?

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.260
You sure you understand computers.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:34.340
But lets take that apart
just a little bit.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:40.880
So if I were majoring in medicine,
well how much experience

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.670
would I have to have in a medical setting
before I could be a medical major?

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:52.540
Or how much engineering experience do
I need before I became an engineer?

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:59.654
Or how much teaching experience would
I need before I became an educator?

00:10:59.654 --> 00:11:04.955
You see, what happens is that we actually
help people figure out how to be a doctor,

00:11:04.955 --> 00:11:07.350
an engineer, a teacher.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:14.150
That's what graduate and
undergraduate education's all about.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:21.747
So the percentage of women went from
7% to 42% with just that one change.

00:11:21.747 --> 00:11:24.880
[MUSIC]

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:28.076
Research was done looking
at organizations, in fact,

00:11:28.076 --> 00:11:31.680
there were three tech organizations and
one consulting firm.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.840
And the researchers got access to all of
the performance reviews from those four

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.080
companies.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:45.420
And they did an analysis of how did those
reviews differ by gender, and if so, how.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:50.200
What they found was,
is that the majority of the reviews

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.540
of males as compared to females actually

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:59.680
associated the review with
a tangible business related outcome.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.460
So the men were told how well or

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:06.970
poorly they did on something that
mattered to the organization.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:07.930
A real business outcome.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:15.220
What was interesting is, for the females,
they were mostly given vague feedback.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:16.450
You're doing okay.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:17.410
You're not doing well.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:21.580
Maybe you need to have this, but
there was no precision in that feedback.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.790
And of the women who were
given more precise feedback,

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:31.960
what was also true was it was mostly
about their communication style.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.970
And this is the fact the wayI
think is the most telling.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:43.030
And looking at all these
performance reviews 75%

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:46.799
of the individuals in these reviews
who were judged to be aggressive.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:52.290
And that aggression came out in
their performance review were women.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:54.740
Seriously?

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:58.690
Of all the people in these organizations,

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:02.410
the problem they were having with
aggression was because of the females?

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.877
Or maybe it's because we have
a different standard for

00:13:06.877 --> 00:13:10.453
what we adjudicate as aggression
in women versus aggression in men.

00:13:10.453 --> 00:13:15.697
There was a study in a medical school that
looked at the letters of recommendation

00:13:15.697 --> 00:13:19.950
for their students who were going
out into residency positions.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:24.760
And they found very interesting results.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:29.670
Number one, the letters of
the male students were much longer

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:32.210
then those of the female.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:38.000
And the letters of the male students
talked about their accomplishments and

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.800
their potential.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:43.070
While the letters of the female students
talked about their personality.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:44.855
[MUSIC]

00:13:44.855 --> 00:13:49.890
Think about an organization
whose culture is strongly

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:55.680
committed to meritocracy and
data-driven decisions.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:00.480
You’d think in that type of organization
a lot of these problems would go away,

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:03.630
but it turns out just
the opposite is true.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:10.345
When an organization has such
a strong adherence to meritocracy and

00:14:10.345 --> 00:14:12.240
data-driven decisions.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:17.370
And their managers and leaders, because of
their association with the organization,

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:20.100
or by explicit statement, agree with that.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:24.040
It turns out those folks are actually more
likely to discriminate against women, and

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:28.220
people of color in their
compensation decisions, why?

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.160
Based on paradoxical, but

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:37.580
it turns out that this is called
the moral licensing effect.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:39.130
So have you ever been a situation?

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.160
Well, happens to me all the time.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:46.000
I go to work out and then because
I've worked out really hard I now

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.700
give myself permission to have
that piece of chocolate cake.

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:53.960
That's called moral licensing.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:56.110
I've already proved
that I'm a good person.

00:14:56.110 --> 00:15:01.503
I worked out and now,
[SOUND] I can eat my cake.

00:15:01.503 --> 00:15:08.570
If I'm a manager in an organization that
has such a strong notion of meritocracy.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.810
Then by definition of being in that
organization, I lower my guard

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:18.050
about whether or not my individual
decision are consistent with that,

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:19.940
because this is part of this organization.

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:28.400
It's our DNA, so
of course we focus on merit and data.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:36.600
This is the problem, organizations and
individuals need to be eternally vigilant.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:40.530
We cannot just assume that that problem
has passed, and we have solved the problem

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:44.450
because we care about data or
that we care about meritocracy.

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:52.540
We have to assess, are the decisions that
I'm making consistent with the data?

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:55.680
Not my perceptions,
not my biases, but the data.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:16:01.110
And more importantly,
do I every day make sure that I actually

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:05.730
broaden the potential of who I might
consider for jobs, rather than narrow?

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.940
Am I taking responsibility?

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:12.200
When I see good performance,
do I let other people know?

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.340
Or do I just let that good
performance speak for

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.760
itself, assuming of course
everyone else will see it.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:24.390
So what is my contribution to the lack
of diversity in my organization?

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:29.242
And that's something that everyone
of us needs to think about because

00:16:29.242 --> 00:16:30.897
it is up to us to change.

00:16:30.897 --> 00:16:34.313
[MUSIC]

